# KNOWN GAP — a closure that CAPTURES a `Callable[T]` PARAMETER cannot be
# compiled: the captured value's env field is typed `unit`, so the generated C
# dereferences a `void` slot.
#
# MECHANISM, read at HEAD (not guessed). A `Callable[T]` parameter's GIR local
# type is ERASED to unit. `ClosureLowering::lower_closure`
# (`src/ir/lowering/closures.rs`) builds the env struct's field types straight
# from each capture's `type_id` (`pointee_type(..).unwrap_or(cap.type_id)`), so
# the field for `f` is `unit` — and loading it emits
#
#     __v4 = (void*)&((__gg___Closure_0 *)(__v3))->f;
#     __v5 = *(void *)(__v4);        <-- error: void value not ignored as it
#                                        ought to be
#
# on C, and `void type only allowed for function results` from llc on LLVM.
# There is a SECOND fault one line further down: the indirect call inside the
# closure body is cast `((int64_t(*)(void*, void*))...)` even for a
# `String`-returning callable, because the enclosing function's
# `callable_return_types` / `callable_param_types` sidecars are not propagated
# into the lifted closure's own `func_state` (inside the closure, `f` is an env
# field, not the outer local the sidecar is keyed on).
#
# ⚠ DISCRIMINATE FROM `t0770` (fixed): that one is about the combinator
# adapter's own reader of the callable's RETURN TYPE, and its reader 5 — the
# closure-body return-type inference in `closures.rs` — IS fixed and DOES fire
# on this program (verified by instrumentation). It is not sufficient: the
# closure's registered return type is re-pinned from the emitted body, and the
# body cannot be emitted at all while the env field is `void`. So this is a
# CAPTURE-TYPING + sidecar-propagation gap, at the closure boundary, not a
# reader defect.
#
# ⚠ DISCRIMINATE FROM `t0526` (closure-env TypeDef built with no drop
# strategy): that one is about the env's DROP, this one about a field's TYPE.
# And from `t0026` (capture of a collection-element borrow returns the
# address): that capture has a real type and compiles.
#
# THE DESIGN QUESTION THE FIX OWES AN ANSWER TO: a callable value is a fat
# pointer (code pointer + environment pointer). Capturing one BY VALUE copies
# the pair, which aliases the captured environment — so the fix has to say
# whether a captured `Callable` is a borrow of the outer callable (lifetime
# tied to it) or an owned copy. That is why this is filed rather than patched.
#
# NEGATIVE CONTROL: the identical program with a TOP-LEVEL FUNCTION in place of
# the captured parameter builds and prints `hello!` on both backends.
#
# INTENDED: prints `hello!`, rc 0, on C and LLVM.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void go(Callable[String(String)] f):
    Callable[String()] g = (): f("hel")
    print(g())

void main():
    go((String s): mk(s, "lo!"))
